WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.640
How can you identify words that sound
the same but have different meanings?

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.200
Here's a question from Marta.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:12.520
How to identify words that have the same pronunciation,

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.280
but they have different meanings?

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.840
We also want your questions.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:23.840
You can send us an email
to learningenglish@bbc.co.uk.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.440
But now onto homonyms.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.280
So, these words are called homonyms.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.640
That means they have the same pronunciation or spelling,

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:36.640
but they have a different meaning.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:37.880
For example,

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:40.520
'new' and 'knew'.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.400
'Bat' and 'bat'.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.000
'Park' and 'park'.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.600
'To' and 'two'.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.360
There are a lot of examples in English.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.480
So how can you know which word they mean?

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.600
Well, always think about the context.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:59.680
If it's dark and you're outside,

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.640
is it more likely to be 'bat' or 'bat'?

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.240
But if you're chatting to friends about sports,

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.080
then it's more likely to be 'bat'.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:14.360
So I go up to bat next and...wow!

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.920
My next tip is to look at the grammar.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.960
For example, the word 'saw' has a lot of meanings.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:30.400
There's 'soar', the verb that means fly
like a bird or a ball through the sky.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.400
There's 'saw', the past tense of the verb 'see',

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.480
and there's 'sore', the adjective, like pain.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:44.680
So when we have 'is sore', the meaning can only be pain,

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.920
because 'sore' is an adjective.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.040
Here, the verb 'is' before it.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.080
Ah, my neck is sore.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.160
And how about 'is soaring'? Well, there,

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.360
it's a verb in the present continuous form.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.040
So the meaning could be 'flying through the sky' – 'is soaring'.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:09.960
Or it could be 'the action using a tool, the saw'.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.840
Wow, the ball is soaring

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.160
out the stadium!

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:18.800
There are lots of homonyms in English,
so learn some and practise using them.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.760
But a lot of the time, many of them will only be used

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:25.040
in very specific situations.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.440
So, for example, 'saw' will usually be
the past tense of 'see'.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:34.120
I saw a really good film last night.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:40.120
'Sore', 'saw' and 'soar' are less common.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.640
Sometimes words put together can sound very similar,

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.600
but they have different meanings.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.040
What do you understand by this?

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:54.480
'Four candles'. You might have heard
'four candles' or 'fork handles'.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.120
They sound very similar.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.840
But again, think about where you are,
who you're with,

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.400
what are they talking about?

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.880
What's most likely to be said in that situation?

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.280
Here are some other examples.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.560
'Grade A'. 'Grey day'.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.320
'I scream'.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:13.640
'Ice cream'.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:15.240
Well, that's all for now,

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:18.520
but check below where there's more
information on this topic

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:21.080
and I will see you soon. Bye!

